  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nBy in large the stagnant pattern continues for us locally through \nthe upcoming week, though there will be some movement to cut back on \nthe extreme blocking. Just as there's some easing in the eastern \ntrough, portions of the western trough eject northeast around the \nridge, cutting back on its intensity and amplitude. The most \nsignificant is on Monday when the core of the western US trough \nexits through Colorado into Minnesota by Tuesday. Guidance is in \ngood agreement that this passes far enough to our north to avoid any \nsignificant impacts locally aside from increased mid and high level \ncloud cover. There is a cold front behind this trough, but it gets \nhung up over Iowa Wednesday into Thursday keeping us warm and dry \nsouth of it.\n\nThere is some restructuring of the pattern from a high amplitude \nridge to a broader, flat topped ridge over much of the central and \neastern US with the jet near the Canadian border. Out west, although \none trough ejects, it's soon replaced by a new one dropping south \nout of Alaska into the Northwest US. While this general idea is \nagreed upon, guidance varies wildly in how this next trough \nprogresses late this week and into the following week. Locally, \nthough, we transition to more of a southwesterly upper level flow \nwith better moisture transport through our region. While the precise \ndetails vary, this does translate to increased rain chances \neventually, just in time for the Halloween weekend. These chances \npeak Saturday into Sunday when at least one shortwave trough is \nexpected to pass near the region. Among the 00Z ensemble guidance, \n80 to 90 percent of members produce measurable rain across our \nentire forecast area in the 24 hours ending at noon on Sunday. This \nindicates strong confidence that at least some rain will fall \nlocally this weekend. Latest NBM PoP peaks at only 60 percent due \nprimarily to timing uncertainty (will it fall Saturday, Saturday \nnight, or Sunday?).\n\nLooking beyond this weekend, there's a general consensus that the \nridge fully breaks down and some flavor of troughing arrives early \nnext week. But whether this represents a return to more \"normal\" \ntemperatures or something even cooler is not well agreed upon.",
